ves  you faith that the federal government  could properly invest money in sectors  that that are gonna grow because we've  seen in the past malinvestment you know  the the Solyndra bust was famously a bad  solar investment the shovel-ready jobs  Obama invested in for the stimulus  weren't exactly shovel ready here in  California we have a huge boondoggle  called the high-speed rail  seems to be going nowhere just by you  know a huge price tag so what you know  so I have some skepticism about the  ability of governments to jumpstart the  economy with investment what gives you  faith that that can actually work yeah  so we have a very corrupted government  yes you know in which lobbyists and the  revolving door rule today so the green  New Deal is protected first of all it's  not a federal cookie cutter program okay  so it provides resources incentives  low-interest loans etc at the local  level so and there it calls for a  process that's firewalled from the  pay-to-play  so if you're part of the pay-to-play  system you cannot participate if you are  providing campaign contributions and  you've got a lobbyist that's that's got  a stake in this then you have basically  exited yourself from participating in  the process so that it's people in the  community coming together to say we need  this kind of stuff mm-hmm to meet the  criteria which is basically making a  more sustainable community in terms of  food energy public transit etc so this  is a decentralized process Stein has  been battling the perception that the  Green Party is anti-vaccine after she  told The Washington Post that there were  real questions that needed to be  addressed I think some of them at least  have been addressed I don't know if all  of them have been addressed in regards  to small amounts of mercury once found  in childhood vaccines despite a  scientific consensus that there's never  been a link between vaccines and autism  or any other serious health problems I  think that's the the birthe